You have started a slow export and want to count the files in its
destination without opening a second terminal. Or perhaps you
interrupted a pager with the wrong key, and the shell insists
there is still a job. The shell can set work aside without losing
track of it, but only within certain boundaries. We will use a
timer and a tiny pipeline, not an export with files at risk.

\begin{objectives}
\item Move a practice job between stopped, foreground, and background states.
\item Distinguish a shell job number from an operating-system PID.
\item Collect a background job's outcome and explain what happens at logout.
\end{objectives}

\section{Who is using the terminal?}
\label{sec:32-foreground}
\index{job control}
\index{foreground}
The shell normally starts a command and waits until it finishes
before printing another prompt. While that command is in the
foreground, keys at the terminal go to the command or to the
terminal line discipline, rather than becoming shell commands.
If a program asks a question, typing \texttt{pwd} answers the
program; it does not get your bearings. The prompt and the
program's own messages are important clues about who is listening.

For this practice use an ordinary interactive Bash or Zsh
in a terminal, not a script, an editor's output pane, or redirected
input. These examples assume their usual job-control settings and
terminal key assignments. On systems where you use a different
shell, open an interactive instance of an available Bash or Zsh.
POSIX specifies a model with job control where supported; it does
not promise it is enabled in every shell context. A shell that
does not respond as described is a reason to stop and check its
manual, not a reason to try random control characters on real work.

\begin{terminal}
sleep 90
\end{terminal}

While the timer is still in the foreground, press
\KeyChord{Ctrl}{Z}. Do not type the letters or press Enter
afterward. On the usual terminal settings, this is the suspend
key. The system sends a stop request to the foreground group of
processes, the timer stops running, and your shell gets the
foreground back. It commonly announces a ``Stopped'' or
``Suspended'' job. If you waited ninety seconds, the timer
probably finished and there is nothing to suspend. If suspend
doesn't work, let this short example expire. Which keys are
assigned to interrupt and suspend can be inspected with
\ManPage{stty}{1}; changing them is not part of this exercise.

\begin{terminal}
jobs
jobs -l
\end{terminal}

\cmd|jobs| lists remembered jobs in this shell. \cmd|-l| requests a
long form including process IDs. Compare your rows to this
illustration, not to its numbers or spacing:

\begin{terminaloutput}
[1]+  7415 Stopped                 sleep 90
\end{terminaloutput}

The number in brackets is the shell's job number. The plus sign
marks the current job, meaning the shell's default choice for
some job commands; it need not be in the foreground now. A minus
sign can mark the previous choice. The larger number in the
illustration is a PID, described in Chapter~\ref{ch:31}. In
this session, \texttt{\%1} would refer to job 1; if your shell
shows a different number, use that number instead. Job numbers
have meaning only to the shell that created them and can be
reused. Don't assume the bracketed numbers will start at 1 if
you already have jobs.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-32/terminal.tex}
\caption{The foreground owner determines who gets your keys.}
\label{fig:32-terminal}
\VisualDescription{A terminal points to the foreground job for
keyboard input. A separate arrow from a background job shows that
it can still produce output.}
\end{figure}

\section{Stop is not finish}
\label{sec:32-resume}
\index{bg@\texttt{bg}}
\index{fg@\texttt{fg}}
\index{jobs@\texttt{jobs}}
The text ``Stopped'' in \cmd|jobs| means the process is suspended,
not that it has exited or that its outputs were saved. It may
still own memory, keep a file open, or keep resources reserved.
A timed wait like \cmd|sleep| can expire while suspended, so
resuming it close to the end may produce a prompt immediately.
Try resuming it in the background while it is still stopped.
Here and in the next examples use \cmd|%1| only if \cmd|jobs|
shows that the timer is job 1.

\begin{terminal}
bg %1
jobs
\end{terminal}

\cmd|bg| resumes a stopped job without giving it the terminal's
foreground. A prompt returns. A row may now say ``Running'',
or ``Done'' if the timer already finished. A finished job can
vanish from the next listing after the shell reports it. If the
timer is still running, try bringing it forward:

\begin{terminal}
fg %1
\end{terminal}

\cmd|fg| gives the job the foreground and waits. To finish
quickly, press \KeyChord{Ctrl}{C} while the running timer still
has the terminal. That is an interrupt, not another suspension.
If the prompt returned before you pressed it, leave the timer
alone: there is nothing left to interrupt. If you accidentally
suspended it again instead, inspect the job and bring it to the
foreground once more, then let it finish naturally. It takes
practice to notice when a prompt has already returned. This is
why a timer is a better target than a file-writing command.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-32/states.tex}
\caption{Suspension changes scheduling; foreground and background
also describe access to the terminal.}
\label{fig:32-states}
\VisualDescription{Foreground and stopped are connected by the
suspend key and fg. Stopped also leads to background via bg.
Background can return to foreground via fg.}
\end{figure}

\begin{quicktable}{M{1in}YY}
\toprule
Action & What changes & What remains true\\
\midrule
Ctrl-Z & The foreground job normally stops running. &
Its processes and resources still exist.\\
bg \%1 & A stopped job resumes in the background. &
It normally cannot read from the terminal.\\
fg \%1 & The job gets the foreground; resume it if stopped. &
The shell waits for the job to stop or exit.\\
Ctrl-C & The terminal normally sends an interrupt to the
foreground group. & Programs can handle interrupts; results
already written stay written.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\section{Send quiet work to the background}
\label{sec:32-background}
\index{background}
\index{wait@\texttt{wait}}
An ampersand at the end of a command tells the shell to start the
command asynchronously, meaning it should not wait for it to
finish before continuing. Unlike \cmd|bg|, this starts new work;
it doesn't resume existing work. Try it here, then read the
two messages in order:

\begin{terminal}
sleep 5 &
timer=$!
printf 'back at the prompt, child %s\n' "$timer"
wait "$timer"
printf 'child exit status: %s\n' "$?"
unset timer
\end{terminal}

The first \cmd|printf| doesn't wait for the timer. The \cmd|wait|
line does, unless the timer already finished. The second
\cmd|printf| reports the outcome of that completed child; expect
zero after an uninterrupted timer. If the shell reports an
invalid or unknown child, check that you issued \cmd|wait| in the
same shell and saved \cmd|$!| immediately. Running many things
in the background and then running plain \cmd|wait| is not a
substitute for checking their individual outcomes: with no
operands, POSIX \cmd|wait| waits for all known children and
returns zero. A shell may also allow a job specification with
its builtin; the simple PID form here is useful in scripts.

For a pipeline the shell can track multiple processes as one job.
Try an example with no files involved; \cmd|cat| just passes
along the timer's empty standard output. It exits when the pipe
closes. Read the \cmd|jobs -l| output before the timer ends.

\begin{terminal}
sleep 15 | cat &
jobs -l
\end{terminal}

The shell describes one job, even when its display names two
processes. To clean up, run \cmd|wait| after making sure there
is no other background work in this practice shell, or let the
fifteen seconds expire. Pipeline status is not automatically
every member's status (see Chapter~\ref{ch:17}); a successful
last stage can conceal an earlier failure. Do not turn a
multi-step data job into a background task just because the
timer example behaves neatly.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-32/pipeline.tex}
\caption{A pipeline may be one shell job and several processes.}
\label{fig:32-pipeline}
\VisualDescription{A dashed job boundary contains separate sleep
and cat nodes. A pipe arrow leads from sleep to cat.}
\end{figure}

Next try a background command that does want input. This example
requires enabled job control and a real terminal. In a script
without job control, an asynchronous command's default input may
instead be assigned from a source like \texttt{/dev/null}.

\begin{terminal}
cat &
jobs
\end{terminal}

If the first \cmd|jobs| still shows ``Running'', wait a moment
and run \cmd|jobs| again; the child may not have attempted the
read yet. A background job that tries to read from the terminal
normally gets stopped by the operating system. Inspect the row:
some shells mention terminal input. To finish, run \cmd|fg|
with that row's job number, then send end-of-input with
\KeyChord{Ctrl}{D} on an empty input line in the usual local
terminal settings. The foreground \cmd|cat| has no more input and
exits. If you had written a partial line, first press Enter;
the end-of-input key doesn't always terminate a nonempty line.
No file is created. If the command was already done, no cleanup
is required.

A background job can usually still write terminal output. The
result can cross the command you're entering, leaving the
transcript hard to read even though the shell's edit buffer
survives. Separate standard output and standard error into
new files when doing this for real, or use a second terminal.
Prefer a job whose required input is specified before launch.
If the tool quietly expects a password or a confirmation, it
is a poor background candidate. Do not redirect unknown prompts
away and later infer that the job succeeded because it never asked
you anything.

\subsection{Keep the output without moving the question}
To see the output behavior without interleaved terminal text,
use the following miniature run. Work from a writable location
and stop after the first line if it doesn't print a new directory.
\cmd|mktemp -d| creates an isolated directory from the template;
it is commonly available but not POSIX. The following grouped
command waits briefly, then writes a fictional completion message.
It has no question to ask. The redirection uses a new name
in that fresh directory so it cannot overwrite a report.

\begin{terminal}
room=$(mktemp -d ./utp-jobs.XXXXXX) && cd "$room" && pwd
\end{terminal}

\begin{terminal}
( sleep 3; printf '%s\n' 'practice finished' ) > ./result.txt &
t=$!
printf '%s\n' 'The shell can continue.'
wait "$t"
printf 'job status: %s\n' "$?"
cat ./result.txt
unset t
\end{terminal}

The parentheses group two commands in a subshell, and the
redirection applies to the group. Immediately after launch
the file can already exist but have nothing in it, since the
shell opens the destination for the command before the useful
output arrives. Wait before using the file as a completed
report. Expect status zero and the single completion line.
The practice group's status is that of the last command
if both are reached; in real data work you should arrange
to test each operation that matters, not just whether a
final message was printed.

To remove this fixture, verify your working directory with
\cmd|pwd|. Confirm the removal only for the one file you
created; the last directory removal refuses nonempty targets.

\begin{terminal}
rm -i ./result.txt
cd .. && rmdir "$room"
unset room
\end{terminal}

For a real long-running task, don't feed a file that is
currently being edited to a job that expects a stable input.
A copy in a working directory makes it clearer which version
the job read. Also decide what should happen to the text sent
to standard error. A run can produce a useful report on
standard output and still print an error about a skipped item.
If you only retrieve the report, you might miss the warning.
Redirect both streams separately when appropriate, using
the rules in Chapter~\ref{ch:16}. A background run isn't less
important just because its messages are out of sight.

\begin{quicktable}{M{1.16in}YY}
\toprule
Context & Useful action & Don't assume\\
\midrule
Same interactive shell & Identify a job with \texttt{jobs}, then
\texttt{fg} or \texttt{bg}. & A job number identifies a global PID.\\
Noninteractive script & Save \texttt{\$!} immediately and
\texttt{wait} for it. & A foreground terminal or a suspend key
is available.\\
Different terminal & Inspect a known process with \texttt{ps}. &
Its original shell's job list moved with you.\\
Closing a session & Check completion or plan for persistence in
advance. & Starting with \texttt{\&} protects work from logout.\\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\begin{dialoguebox}{Did I give it another room?}
\Brian{If the job is in the background, where do its messages go?}
\Stephen{I should have said it runs without the foreground, not without the terminal.}
\Brian{So my export can still print over the line I'm typing?}
\Stephen{Usually, yes. Input and output are different privileges here.}
\Brian{And if it asks me to confirm an overwrite?}
\Stephen{It can't usually read that answer in the background; it may stop.}
\Brian{Then I'd rather keep questions in front of me.}
\Stephen{Right. Give quiet, prepared work to the background.}
\DialogueConclusion{Background work can still speak.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{The boundary at the session door}
\label{sec:32-logout}
Before closing a terminal, run \cmd|jobs| and look for work you
expect to finish. The shell may warn about stopped jobs. Treat it
as a chance to return to them, not a warning to silence. What
happens on terminal closure depends on the shell, job state,
terminal and system setup. In particular, some processes receive
a hangup signal and terminate. Others may continue without usable
input or output. Neither is the equivalent of closing a document
after saving. A second shell can't take over job control merely
because it sees a PID. Persistence needs planning, such as a
terminal multiplexer discussed in Chapter~\ref{ch:40}, or a
documented service intended for scheduled work. Even those do not
protect a task against machine shutdown.

Here is a final harmless check: open another terminal with a
new shell while the practice timer is running. Run \cmd|jobs|
there. The new shell may have jobs of its own, but does not inherit
the timer's job entry. Return to the original shell to collect
the timer. You now have an explanation for a common mismatch:
\cmd|ps| can show that a process exists while \cmd|jobs| says
nothing about it.

If you wanted to continue pressing keys inside the original
program, the distinction matters more than the numbers. A
second terminal is excellent when you only want to look
something up; you can leave the first program in front of
its original terminal. Suspending it is better when you
need to make a brief observation in exactly that shell
and you can safely pause the program. Backgrounding it
is better only if its work can proceed without a question.
Closing its terminal is a fourth action with different
risks. In particular, a background downloader or converter
may hold files open and continue using storage even without
any visible window. When a result turns out incomplete,
collect the status before closing the shell if you still
can. This information helps you decide whether to retry with a
new output name or whether there is completion evidence to keep.

\begin{exercise}{Interrupt or suspend?}
\label{ex:32-suspend}
Start \cmd|sleep 30|, suspend it immediately, then use a job
listing to verify that it hasn't exited. Bring it back to the
foreground and let it finish.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Use \KeyChord{Ctrl}{Z}, then \cmd|jobs|. Look for a stopped or
suspended row for the timer. With that row's number, for example
1, use \cmd|fg %1|. Wait for the prompt. Run \cmd|jobs| again
to verify there is no stopped timer. Nothing was written to disk.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Wait in the right shell}
\label{ex:32-wait}
Start a five-second background timer, save its child ID, and
verify that counting characters is possible before collecting
the timer. Then print the timer's exit status.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
sleep 5 &
t=$!
printf 'clock\n' | wc -c
wait "$t"
printf 'status: %s\n' "$?"
unset t
\end{terminal}
The byte count is six, with the newline included. The timer exits
with status zero if uninterrupted. Cleanup consists of waiting and
dropping the variable, not closing the terminal.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Follow the pipeline as one job}
\label{ex:32-pipeline}
Repeat the fifteen-second pipeline. Identify its job number and
use the long listing to look for evidence of separate processes.
Bring the job to the foreground and let it end, or let the
timer expire if you took longer than fifteen seconds.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
Run \cmd+sleep 15 | cat &+, then \cmd|jobs -l|. The formatting
is shell-specific; in Bash the long form commonly shows separate
PID lines for the two members. Use \cmd|fg %1| if the job is
still running and the selected number really is 1. After it
exits no files or processes from the exercise need cleanup.
\end{solution}

\begin{takeaways}
\item A job belongs to one shell and may contain more than one process.
\item Suspend, resume, interrupt, and wait are different actions.
\item A background job is not a persistent session or a guarantee
of successful completion.
\end{takeaways}

